WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.900
So, I know that we've gone through all this and some of you might be thinking,

00:03.900 --> 00:06.820
well, who builds web apps this way?

00:06.820 --> 00:10.920
Because after all, every modern text that you'll see always talks about

00:10.920 --> 00:12.780
build tools and that kind of thing.

00:12.780 --> 00:16.820
To be honest, progressive enhancement is very much a thing that

00:16.820 --> 00:19.340
continues to exist to this day even though it's not talked about a lot.

00:19.340 --> 00:20.980
Code read writes are very sexy,

00:20.980 --> 00:24.360
but the truth is when you're dealing with enterprise systems,

00:24.360 --> 00:29.300
it's just not realistic to stop everything as we talked about before and write new features.

00:29.300 --> 00:31.580
But dropping in the CDN with view,

00:31.580 --> 00:34.380
having it progressively enhanced, that all works.

00:34.380 --> 00:38.060
Because we're not going to cover it at this point because we're going to move into build tools.

00:38.060 --> 00:39.900
But a lot of these things we're seeing here,

00:39.900 --> 00:42.580
they can be just abstracted into .js files.

00:42.580 --> 00:44.660
You can actually modularize your code.

00:44.660 --> 00:48.060
You can do a lot actually and make a fairly robust app.

00:48.060 --> 00:52.780
It doesn't have to all live inside of this single object that I've shown you all.

00:52.780 --> 00:54.900
That's just a way for us to keep it all as a mental model.

00:54.900 --> 00:56.700
But eventually, you can break it out,

00:56.700 --> 01:02.060
import .js into here, use it like it can still scale pretty darn well.

01:02.060 --> 01:05.460
So this image here, which I love is like the cake,

01:05.460 --> 01:07.660
that then you get the frosting, then you get the candles.

01:07.660 --> 01:10.380
So the credit here is a Tiffany say,

01:10.380 --> 01:13.700
this is our blog post on what is progressive enhancement and why should you care.

01:13.700 --> 01:16.300
So definitely something that I do think modern web developers need to

01:16.300 --> 01:22.060
consider when it comes to the realities of upgrading their code base.

01:22.260 --> 01:26.740
So this is a smashing article magazine by

01:26.740 --> 01:29.460
Sarah Drasner who used to be on the View Core team.

01:29.460 --> 01:32.060
This is a great one because the title of this is

01:32.060 --> 01:35.100
Replacing jQuery with View.js, no build step necessary.

01:35.100 --> 01:37.780
This is also a very valid way of using View.

01:37.780 --> 01:40.380
It's like if you know that only one page on your entire app,

01:40.380 --> 01:43.260
because you're statically generating it so everything's on the server side,

01:43.260 --> 01:45.500
but only one could use some interactivity,

01:45.500 --> 01:47.180
don't even need jQuery anymore.

01:47.180 --> 01:50.380
You could literally just drop View because I believe the CDN site is either roughly the same,

01:50.380 --> 01:53.180
if View is not slightly lower at this point,

01:53.180 --> 01:55.220
but that is actually a viable option now.

01:55.220 --> 01:57.060
You don't need to even approach View as a framework.

01:57.060 --> 02:01.460
You can be just little bits of interactivity that are sprinkled in an app.

02:01.700 --> 02:05.040
Finally, again, people always get the idea like,

02:05.040 --> 02:06.940
well, who's actually done it this way?

02:06.940 --> 02:09.060
Well, GitLab did it this way.

02:09.060 --> 02:11.820
GitLab used to actually be a Ruby on Rails app.

02:11.820 --> 02:14.100
It was super-length. So if anyone works with Ruby on Rails,

02:14.100 --> 02:15.620
you know that especially back then,

02:15.620 --> 02:21.580
integrating node-based tool chains into that was extremely painful.

02:21.580 --> 02:23.860
Again, and also they needed to ship features.

02:23.860 --> 02:25.140
This was before they went public.

02:25.140 --> 02:27.860
They had customers they needed to meet the needs.

02:27.860 --> 02:29.820
So they couldn't stop everything and go,

02:29.820 --> 02:31.660
yeah, Rails is outdated.

02:31.660 --> 02:32.740
We really would love to use a framework,

02:32.740 --> 02:33.700
so let's stop building stuff.

02:33.700 --> 02:35.540
They couldn't do that. So what did they do?

02:35.540 --> 02:38.380
I'm trying to remember actually which page it was.

02:38.380 --> 02:40.580
I knew someone who was on the team that did this.

02:40.580 --> 02:42.420
Let's just, for example,

02:42.420 --> 02:45.020
they had the sign-in page and they said on the sign-in page,

02:45.060 --> 02:48.900
let's add view as a CDN and let's just add interactivity.

02:48.900 --> 02:50.460
Once they proved that that worked well,

02:50.460 --> 02:51.740
they went, okay, next one.

02:51.740 --> 02:54.060
What's something we can hit that's fairly low-impact

02:54.060 --> 02:55.540
performance-wise that we can do?

02:55.540 --> 02:57.660
I think there was another one that they did.

02:57.660 --> 02:58.820
Basically, that's what they did.

02:58.820 --> 03:01.300
They slowly added new features to the customer base

03:01.300 --> 03:03.860
while progressively migrating their app to view.

03:03.860 --> 03:09.420
Then eventually, when they had the tipping point of like,

03:09.420 --> 03:12.700
they had so much code in view that it made sense to like,

03:12.820 --> 03:15.100
let's take a couple of weeks to like refactor our code

03:15.100 --> 03:18.380
to actually use the node tooling to like bring it all in.

03:18.380 --> 03:19.380
That's when they actually did that,

03:19.380 --> 03:22.460
but they proved their case rather than trying to just tell product,

03:22.460 --> 03:24.660
hey, just stop. We have this wonderful idea that

03:24.660 --> 03:28.180
if anyone has done ever a massive app refactoring,

03:28.180 --> 03:30.620
it often doesn't go quite as planned.

03:30.620 --> 03:32.060
Then deadlines get pushed,

03:32.060 --> 03:34.700
but they were able to realistic do that.

03:34.700 --> 03:37.260
And so that's how GitLab is now running on view now.

03:37.260 --> 03:40.140
And so the reason I wanted to emphasize this method is

03:40.140 --> 03:42.460
because it's something that I think is often overlooked

03:42.460 --> 03:45.060
when people are thinking about recommending solutions to clients.

03:45.060 --> 03:47.780
And it's something that I want people to know about

03:47.780 --> 03:49.100
so whenever you're working for someone

03:49.100 --> 03:50.940
or you think that this could be a good approach,

03:50.940 --> 03:53.060
you now know that this is here for you.

